<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chach Nama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Fathnama Sindh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Chach</a> Nama is a Persian chronicle of the Arab conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, translated by Ali Kufi around 1226 from what it claims was an earlier Arabic work. It tells of the Rai dynasty, the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> chamberlain Chach and the fall of his son Dahir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a> in 712. It is the main narrative source for early medieval Sindh.</p></div>
